## Proof

Let $(\mathbb{R}^2, g)$ be the plane with the standard Euclidean metric $g = dx^2 + dy^2$, and let $(x, y)$ be the standard global coordinates with coordinate vector fields $\partial_x, \partial_y$.

**Definition of the connection.** Define an affine connection $\nabla$ on $\mathbb{R}^2$ by specifying its action on the coordinate frame:

$$
\nabla_{\partial_x} \partial_x = \partial_y, \qquad
\nabla_{\partial_x} \partial_y = -\partial_x, \qquad
\nabla_{\partial_y} \partial_x = 0, \qquad
\nabla_{\partial_y} \partial_y = 0,
$$

and extend to all vector fields by $\mathbb{R}$-linearity and the Leibniz rule $\nabla_{fX} Y = f\,\nabla_X Y$ and $\nabla_X(fY) = X(f)\,Y + f\,\nabla_X Y$. Equivalently, the only nonzero Christoffel symbols are

$$
\Gamma^{y}_{xx} = 1, \qquad \Gamma^{x}_{xy} = -1,
$$

with all other $\Gamma^k_{ij} = 0$.

**$\nabla$ is metric.** We verify the metric-compatibility condition
$$
X\langle Y, Z\rangle = \langle \nabla_X Y,\, Z\rangle + \langle Y,\, \nabla_X Z\rangle
$$
for all $X, Y, Z \in \{\partial_x, \partial_y\}$. Since $g$ is the Euclidean metric, $\langle \partial_i, \partial_j\rangle = \delta_{ij}$ is constant, so the left-hand side is always $0$. We check the right-hand side vanishes in every case:

| $X$ | $Y$ | $Z$ | $\langle \nabla_X Y, Z\rangle + \langle Y, \nabla_X Z\rangle$ |
|-----|-----|-----|---|
| $\partial_x$ | $\partial_x$ | $\partial_x$ | $\langle \partial_y, \partial_x\rangle + \langle \partial_x, \partial_y\rangle = 0 + 0 = 0$ |
| $\partial_x$ | $\partial_x$ | $\partial_y$ | $\langle \partial_y, \partial_y\rangle + \langle \partial_x, -\partial_x\rangle = 1 - 1 = 0$ |
| $\partial_x$ | $\partial_y$ | $\partial_x$ | $\langle -\partial_x, \partial_x\rangle + \langle \partial_y, \partial_y\rangle = -1 + 1 = 0$ |
| $\partial_x$ | $\partial_y$ | $\partial_y$ | $\langle -\partial_x, \partial_y\rangle + \langle \partial_y, -\partial_x\rangle = 0 + 0 = 0$ |
| $\partial_y$ | $\partial_x$ | $\partial_x$ | $\langle 0, \partial_x\rangle + \langle \partial_x, 0\rangle = 0$ |
| $\partial_y$ | $\partial_x$ | $\partial_y$ | $\langle 0, \partial_y\rangle + \langle \partial_x, 0\rangle = 0$ |
| $\partial_y$ | $\partial_y$ | $\partial_x$ | $\langle 0, \partial_x\rangle + \langle \partial_y, 0\rangle = 0$ |
| $\partial_y$ | $\partial_y$ | $\partial_y$ | $\langle 0, \partial_y\rangle + \langle \partial_y, 0\rangle = 0$ |

All cases give $0$, so $\nabla$ is metric-compatible: $\nabla g = 0$.

**$\nabla$ is not torsion-free.** The torsion tensor is $T(X, Y) = \nabla_X Y - \nabla_Y X - [X, Y]$. For the coordinate fields, $[\partial_x, \partial_y] = 0$, so

$$
T(\partial_x, \partial_y) = \nabla_{\partial_x}\partial_y - \nabla_{\partial_y}\partial_x = (-\partial_x) - 0 = -\partial_x \neq 0.
$$

Equivalently, $T^x_{xy} = \Gamma^x_{xy} - \Gamma^x_{yx} = -1 - 0 = -1 \neq 0$, confirming the torsion does not vanish.

**Conclusion.** The connection $\nabla$ defined above satisfies $\nabla g = 0$ (metric) but has nonzero torsion $T(\partial_x, \partial_y) = -\partial_x$ (not torsion-free). Hence it is an affine connection on $(\mathbb{R}^2, \langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle)$ that is metric but not torsion-free. $\blacksquare$

### PROOF COMPLETE
